      en: "The National Palace of the Dominican Republic in Santo Domingo is the seat of the executive power and the official office of the president. This monumental building was constructed during the era of Rafael Trujillo and inaugurated on August 16, 1947. Designed by Italian architect Guido D'Alessandro, the palace features a grand Neoclassical style intended to project a sense of authority and national stability. Spanning approximately 18,000 square meters, the structure is renowned for its massive central dome and the extensive use of domestic marble and fine mahogany in its interior. The halls are decorated with magnificent murals by artists like Aurelioocigatti, depicting pivotal scenes from Dominican history. Perched on a hill in the Gazcue sector, the palace is surrounded by manicured gardens and guarded by a permanent military honor guard. It is widely considered one of the most architecturally significant government buildings in the Caribbean region. While it primarily serves administrative functions, the palace is also the venue for state visits and major national ceremonies. Its design harmoniously blends European influences with local materials, symbolizing the sovereignty of the Dominican state.",
